<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    This file exists for two reasons, and the second is the one that was not obvious.

    It merges WinUI's default style dictionary, which is what gives controls their Fluent
    appearance; without it a Button renders as a flat square grey rectangle and nothing
    errors, because an unstyled control is still a valid control.

    And its presence is what causes an app resources.pri to be generated at all. The PRI
    targets index the project's XAML; a project with no XAML files indexes nothing and
    produces no app resource map. XamlControlsResources then cannot load its own dictionary
    and the process dies with 0xC000027B, a stowed WinRT exception, which is exactly what
    happened when this was attempted from C# instead, first in the App constructor and then
    in OnLaunched.

    So the missing PRI and the unstyled controls were the same problem after all, and the
    order of causation runs the other way from the one assumed: no XAML meant no PRI, and no
    PRI meant no styles.
-->
<Application
    x:Class="Cn1NativeRef.App"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:controls="using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Controls">
    <Application.Resources>
        <ResourceDictionary>
            <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
                <controls:XamlControlsResources />
            </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>

            <!--
                The tile backdrop, and it has to be declared HERE rather than assigned in
                code. Application.Current.Resources["SolidBackgroundFillColorBaseBrush"]
                resolves against the application dictionary once, with no element-theme
                resolution, so a tile whose RequestedTheme is Dark still got the LIGHT
                brush: the dark half of the set was captured as dark controls on the light
                #F3F3F3 surface, which is a backdrop no Windows 11 app ever shows.

                A Setter with {ThemeResource} is resolved when the style is applied, against
                the element's effective theme, which is the behaviour this needs.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="TileHostStyle" TargetType="Grid">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource SolidBackgroundFillColorBaseBrush}" />
            </Style>

            <!--
                The separator rule and the group box frame, here for exactly the reason the
                tile backdrop is: both were assigned in code from
                Application.Current.Resources, which is the one-shot application-dictionary
                read described above.

                The symptom is quieter than the backdrop's was, which is why it survived a
                review round. In light mode the brushes happen to be right. In dark mode the
                captured rule was #1E1E1E on a #202020 tile and the frame the same, DARKER
                than the surface behind them, when both of these resources are a translucent
                WHITE in the dark theme and must come out lighter. What was actually captured
                is the LIGHT theme's translucent black composited onto the dark tile:
                0x20 * (1 - 15/255) is 0x1E, to the number. The result reads as a blank tile.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="SeparatorRuleStyle" TargetType="Border">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource DividerStrokeColorDefaultBrush}" />
            </Style>

            <Style x:Key="GroupBoxFrameStyle" TargetType="Border">
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource CardStrokeColorDefaultBrush}" />
            </Style>
        </ResourceDictionary>
    </Application.Resources>
</Application>
